  "notes": "3 tricks are available from each of hearts, diamonds, and clubs. 4 tricks are thus required in spades. If spades break 2-2 or 3-1, the ♠J and ♠10 will fall under the ♠A, ♠K, and ♠Q making the ♠9 high. West's heart opening lead is won in dummy.\n\nWhen East has 4 spades, it is necessary to cash dummy's ♠A first. West shows out marking the 4-0 break. A spade is now played through East. East may play his ♠J or ♠10 but he is still helpless. South can cross to dummy (it is important not to have cashed all of the hearts and clubs) and a finessing position remains over East's ♠J.\n\nNotice that if the ♠K or ♠Q is played first East will always take a spade trick by splitting his honours when a spade is led towards South.\n\nThere is no winning play if West has 4 spades. West can simply cover whenever South leads the ♠9 or ♠8 towards dummy, thus maintaining a sure stopper.\n\nTherefore, the correct play is to lead the ♠A from North on the first round of the suit, guarding against East having all 4 spades.",
